    "description": "Prague is often called one of Europe’s most mysterious cities, and its fascination with alchemy dates back to the reign of Emperor Rudolf II, who invited famous alchemists, astrologers, and scholars to his court during the Renaissance.\r\n\r\nToday, visitors can explore this fascinating world through museums such as Speculum Alchemiae and Museum of Alchemists and Magicians of Old Prague, both located in historic buildings connected to Prague’s alchemical legends.\r\n\r\nThe museums offer immersive experiences featuring reconstructed medieval laboratories, underground tunnels, mysterious artifacts, алхymic instruments, and interactive exhibits exploring the search for the Philosopher’s Stone and the Elixir of Life.\r\n\r\nVisitors can discover the stories of famous historical figures such as Edward Kelley and John Dee while experiencing the mystical atmosphere that made Prague the “capital of alchemy” in the 16th century. With hidden passages, ancient spiral staircases, and candlelit interiors, these museums combine history, science, and legend into one of Prague’s most unique cultural attractions. \r\n\r\nVisit can be enchnated by special Alchymists drinks in cafe.",
