    "description": "Copenhagen City Hall (Danish: Københavns Rådhus) is the headquarters of the Copenhagen City Council as well as the Lord mayor of the Copenhagen Municipality, Denmark. \r\n\r\nThe building is situated on City Hall Square in central Copenhagen. \r\n\r\nThe current building was inaugurated in 1905. It was designed by the architect Martin Nyrop in the National Romantic style but with inspiration from the Siena City Hall. It is dominated by its richly ornamented front, the gilded statue of Absalon just above the balcony and the tall, slim clock tower. The latter is, at 105.6 metres, one of the tallest buildings in the generally low city of Copenhagen.\r\n\r\nIn addition to the tower clock, the City Hall also houses Jens Olsen's World Clock. \r\n\r\nJens Olsen's World Clock was designed and calculated by Jens Olsen (1872–1945), who was a skilled locksmith, and later learned the trade of clockmaking.",
    "tourProgramExample": "Hearing about the Copenhagen City Council system and each policy, facility visit\r\n\r\nMorning\r\n10:00 Meet and greet at the main hall\r\n10:15-11:00 Presentations from City Hall and Copenhagen City Administration\r\n11:00-11:45 Guided tour of Copenhagen City Hall　　\r\n11:45-12:00 Snacks (Coffee & City Hall Pancakes)\r\nafternoon",
