    "description": "Christiansborg castle is the former residence of the Danish Royal family and is now home to the Danish Parliment, the Supreme Court and the Ministry of State.\r\n\r\nVistors can walk through different areas of the castle: \r\n\r\nRoyal Reception Rooms- In the Royal Reception Rooms, the Queen of Denmark receives foreign kings, queens and presidents. The people are received in Audience, gala dinners are hosted and new laws are signed by the Queen\r\n\r\nThe Royal Stables- There have been horses in the Royal Stables since 1740. The stable complex and the two stable wing buildings (which also include the Court Theatre) are the only buildings to survive from Christian VI’s pompous Baroque palace\r\n\r\nThe Royal Kitchen- Visitors are given exclusive access behind the scenes and they see the preparations for royal banquets in the newly refurbished Royal Kitchen, which boasts one of Europe's largest collections of copperware\r\n\r\nThe ruins under Christiansborg Palace- The largest and oldest ruin under the palace is the curtain wall from Bishop Absalon’s 12th century castle. The curtain wall protected the castle from Wendish pirates that attacked Copenhagen from the south\r\n\r\nChristiansborg Palace Chapel- The Palace Chapel which dates from 1826 is used for royal events such as weddings, baptisms and anniversaries. The chapel’s first major royal event was Frederick VII’s and Princess Vilhelmine’s magnificent wedding in 1828",
