    "description": "Palazzo Chiaramonte-Steri reflects the political, cultural, and dramatic history of Sicily from the Middle Ages to the Spanish period.\r\n\r\nBuilt in the early 14th century (1307–1320) by the Chiaramonte family, over centuries the palace served as a viceroyal residence, customs office, and tribunal of the Inquisition. Today, it houses the official headquarters (Rectorate) of the University of Palermo.\r\n\r\nVisitors can explore the rich artistic and historical layers that tell the story of political power and social control in medieval and early modern Sicily including the Sala Magna (Hall of the Barons) main ceremonial hall with magnificent 14th-century painted wooden ceiling used today for graduations, conferences, and institutional meetings.\r\n\r\nThe attached small Gothic church of St. Anthony Abate adds a sacred dimension to the site’s cultural heritage.",
    "tourProgramExample": "- Combines medieval, Spanish and modern university history\r\n- Sala Magna (Hall of the Barons) main ceremonial \r\nhall \r\n- A symbol of Palermo’s \r\ncultural identity",
