    "id": "mice-aGFja25leSBob21lcw",
    "title": "Hackney Homes",
    "category": "Technical Visit",
    "targetTourCategories": [],
    "tags": [
      "Administration"
    ],
    "description": "No record of investigation or arrangement (as information) *********\r\n\r\nEstablished in April 2006, it is an NPO entrusted with the management, repair, maintenance and operation of public housing in the London borough of Hackney.\r\n\r\n< comments>\r\nThe Labour government has set a goal of ensuring that all municipality-owned public housing meets \"unsightly housing standards\" by 2010.\r\nHowever, in order to achieve this, funds must be raised to repair a large number of unrepaired areas of public housing.\r\nThe government \"will not relax the rules that allow municipalities to borrow more money to pay for repairs.\r\nIf municipalities want to secure funds to repair unrepaired areas, they must consider three options.\" He declared.\r\nThe first is to transfer public housing from local governments to housing associations, which are private non-profit companies, and the cooperatives borrow funds.\r\nThe second is the PFI (Private Finance Initiative) system, in which the private sector improves housing services.\r\nThe third is the ALMO model, which creates a separate organization to operate public housing instead of a local government.\r\n\"There is no fourth option in public housing operations to maintain the status quo and secure additional funding for repairs,\" the government said, and municipalities must consider adopting one of three options to achieve the 2010 home repair targets.\r\n(From the materials issued in January 2004 by the Council of Local Authorities for International Relations)\r\n\r\n---------------------------\r\n\r\nHackney Homes is the third choice ALMO (the Arm's Length Management Organization).\r\n\r\n< comments>\r\nThe ALMO model is currently being reviewed by the Audit Committee.\r\nThe Audit Committee is a monitoring body that evaluates the expenditure and financial efficiency of local governments and reports to the legislature.\r\nIn the report \"Learning from the First ALMO Model\" issued by the Audit Committee in May 2003,\r\nIt provides an overview of how local governments that have introduced ALMO are promoting housing policies.\r\nAccording to the report, municipalities that have generally introduced ALMO have been given freedom to operate public housing.\r\nHousing is well maintained, and appropriate business operations are carried out that are acceptable to the public.\r\nIn addition, ALMO's organizational structure allows tenants to participate in decision-making in the operation of the home.\r\nIt says it is increasing the opportunities to express opinions.\r\nThe report also recommends that local governments continuously review their relationships with ALMO.\r\nCare should be taken to ensure that ALMO staff are provided with all the necessary support.\r\n(From the May 2003 materials issued by the Council of Local Authorities for International Relations)",
    "tourProgramExample": "＜GRQ TYO-0185'09 APARTMENT HOUSE MANAGEMENT IN LON, PAR + SCANDINAVIA＞\r\n\r\nInformational only. \r\nThere is no evidence of investigation.",
