    "description": "Information only (no inspection arrangements)***\r\n\r\nThe Laban Centre is a dance educational institution founded by Rudolf Laban, one of the pioneers of modern dance in Europe, in exile in London during World War II.\r\n\r\nThe building of this prestigious dance school was designed by Swiss architects Herzog and de Meuron, who designed the Tate Modern and the national stadium (commonly known as the Bird's Nest) for the 2008 Beijing Olympics. Herzog & de Meuron completed this bold and iconic building in collaboration with visual artist Michael Craig Martin.\r\nThe outer walls are made of colored translucent polycarbonate and are inlaid with transparent large windows. The building is covered with this polycarbonate translucent lime, magenta and turquoise colors.\r\n\r\nThe interior is designed in the image of a cityscape. There are several corridors, courtyards, meeting places and, in the center, a circular main theater.\r\n\r\nThe center is designed to create a space that reflects the overflowing movements of dance and inspires creativity, to create a place where teachers and students from different disciplines can meet and actively exchange ideas, and to preserve the chaos and informality of the previous Laban Center.\r\n\r\n*The general public is only allowed to enter some parts of the café, Pilates studio, health care room, etc.\r\n　(As of 2008, open only on Mondays, Saturdays, and Sundays.) ）\r\n　\r\n* For groups, guided tours are also available with advance reservation.\r\n　Price: GBP 10 per person \r\n　Number of people: Minimum 10~50 people　\r\n　　　 (Up to 25 people per group, if there are more than 26 people, you will be divided into two groups)",
